  99:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****         case NETWORK_MESSAGE_I_AM_ROUTER_TO_NETWORK:
 100:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****             /* Per the standard, we are supposed to process this message and
 101:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * add its DNETs to our routing table.
 102:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * However, since we only have one upstream port that these
 103:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * messages can come from and replies go to, it doesn't seem
 104:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * to provide us any value to do this; when we need to send to
 105:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * some remote device, we will start by pushing it out the
 106:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * upstream port and let the attached router(s) take it from there.
 107:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * Consequently, we'll do nothing interesting here.
 108:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * -- Unless we act upon NETWORK_MESSAGE_ROUTER_BUSY_TO_NETWORK
 109:../src/middleware/bacnet/demo/handler/h_routed_npdu.c ****              * later for congestion control - then it could matter.
